<!-- ================= SECTION 1 — BEYOND THE WORK ORDER ================= -->
<section class="twsSec_wrap twsSec_section">
  <!-- Row 1: title only -->
  <div class="twsSec_head twsSec_reveal">
    <p class="twsSec_eyebrow">Our Approach</p>
    <h2 class="twsSec_h2">Maintenance That Looks Beyond the Work Order</h2>
  </div>

  <!-- Row 2: image LEFT / content RIGHT -->
  <div class="twsSec_grid twsSec_reveal">
    <div class="twsSec_media">
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--tl"></span>
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--br"></span>
      <img class="twsSec_img" src="https://www.twsss.com.au/_astro/our-faqs-img.yp8Kqpo2.webp" alt="TWS technician inspecting a facility properly">
    </div>
    <div class="twsSec_body">
      <p class="twsSec_tag">The difference shows in the details.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">A contractor who only fixes what is written on the work order may miss the cracked seal above the same doorway. Someone sent to patch paint may ignore the damp mark forming behind it. A technician rushing through a checklist may tick the box and leave the actual risk sitting there for the next person to inherit. That is where maintenance starts becoming expensive.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_sub">We prefer to look properly.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">That does not mean over-servicing. There is enough waste in this industry already. Replacing parts too early, quoting full refurbishments when a controlled repair would hold, sending three trades when one capable technician could have handled the job &mdash; none of that helps the client. Good maintenance is not always the biggest solution. Quite often it is the right small action done before the building forces a larger one.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">TWS Service &amp; Solutions provides integrated facility maintenance across commercial, residential, retail, hospitality, healthcare, education, and mixed-use properties. The work can be routine, reactive, preventative, or project-based. Plumbing issues, electrical coordination, carpentry repairs, painting, pressure cleaning, general handyman works, make-good tasks, minor refurbishments, safety fixes, site presentation, and ongoing property upkeep all sit inside the same real-world problem: keeping a place functional without turning every issue into a drama.</p>
      <a class="twsSec_cta" href="https://www.twsss.com.au/services/integrated-maintenance-services">
        Explore our services
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
(function(){
  var els=document.querySelectorAll('.twsSec_reveal');
  if(!('IntersectionObserver' in window)){els.forEach(function(e){e.classList.add('twsSec_in');});return;}
  var io=new IntersectionObserver(function(entries){
    entries.forEach(function(en){if(en.isIntersecting){en.target.classList.add('twsSec_in');io.unobserve(en.target);}});
  },{threshold:.12});
  els.forEach(function(e){io.observe(e);});
})();
</script>



<!-- ================= SECTION 2 — WORKING AROUND PEOPLE (GREEN BAND) ================= -->
<section class="twsSec_wrap twsSec_section twsSec_section--green">
  <!-- Row 1: text LEFT / image RIGHT -->
  <div class="twsSec_grid twsSec_grid--reverse twsSec_grid--top twsSec_reveal" style="margin-bottom:clamp(40px,5vw,72px);">
    <div class="twsSec_media">
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--br"></span>
      <img class="twsSec_img" src="About_image/res21.jpg" alt="TWS working carefully around an occupied site">
    </div>
    <div class="twsSec_body">
      <p class="twsSec_eyebrow twsSec_eyebrow--gold">On Occupied Sites</p>
      <h2 class="twsSec_h2">Working Around People, Not Just Property</h2>
      <p class="twsSec_tag">That sounds simple until the site is occupied.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">Work around tenants is different. So is work around patients, guests, students, staff, retail customers, or residents who have already complained twice. A good technician needs more than tools. They need timing, restraint, clean work habits, communication, and enough sense to know when noise, dust, access, smell, or disruption will become the real problem.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">That is rarely discussed in maintenance brochures.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Row 2: image LEFT / text RIGHT -->
  <div class="twsSec_grid twsSec_grid--top twsSec_reveal">
    <div class="twsSec_media">
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--tl"></span>
      <img class="twsSec_img" src="About_image/res22.jpg" alt="TWS technician attending a metro maintenance call">
    </div>
    <div class="twsSec_body">
      <p class="twsSec_p">We have seen clients get burned by the cheapest attendance fee. It looks attractive at first. Then the same issue returns, photos are poor, reports say almost nothing, no one owns the outcome, and the invoice trail becomes longer than the repair itself. Cheap work can still be good work. Expensive work can still be lazy. Price alone is a poor filter. The better question is whether the contractor understands the building, the urgency, the people affected, and the consequence of getting it wrong.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">As a <b> facility maintenance company Sydney</b> clients may need for fast metro response, TWS Service &amp; Solutions is built around practical attendance, clear communication, and work that stands up after the technician leaves. Not perfect promises. Buildings are too unpredictable for that. Some leaks only show in wind-driven rain. Some drainage faults behave well during inspection and fail under load. Some old fittings cannot be repaired cleanly because the surrounding material has already reached the end of its life.</p>
      <a class="twsSec_cta twsSec_cta--gold" href="https://www.twsss.com.au/contact">
        Talk to our team
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- ================= SECTION 3 — HONEST REPAIRS & CLEAR REPORTING ================= -->
<section class="twsSec_wrap twsSec_section twsSec_section--tint">
  <!-- Row 1: image LEFT / text RIGHT -->
  <div class="twsSec_grid twsSec_grid--top twsSec_reveal" style="margin-bottom:clamp(40px,5vw,72px);">
    <div class="twsSec_media">
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--tl"></span>
      <img class="twsSec_img" src="About_image/res23.png" alt="TWS clear reporting and honest assessment on site">
    </div>
    <div class="twsSec_body">
      <p class="twsSec_eyebrow">Honesty First</p>
      <h2 class="twsSec_h2">Honest Repairs and Clear Reporting</h2>
      <p class="twsSec_tag">A maintenance partner should say that early.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">There are jobs where a patch is sensible. There are jobs where patching only buys resentment. There are also sites where the recommended repair will fail because the underlying design is wrong, water is travelling from another area, previous work has trapped moisture, or access is too limited to complete the repair properly. We would rather have that conversation before the invoice, not after the complaint.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">That is why reporting matters. Not long reports written to protect nobody. Useful reports. Photos that show the issue clearly. Notes that explain what was done, what was not done, what needs watching, and when a larger decision may be needed. Property managers do not need vague phrases like &ldquo;all completed&rdquo; when the job has conditions attached. Owners do not need panic language when the issue is manageable.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">A <a href="https://www.twsss.com.au/contact"  style="text-decoration: none;    color: black;"> <b>facility maintenance company NSW</b></a> clients rely on should reduce uncertainty, not create more admin.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Row 2: text LEFT / image RIGHT -->
  <div class="twsSec_grid twsSec_grid--reverse twsSec_grid--top twsSec_reveal">
    <div class="twsSec_media">
      <span class="twsSec_blob twsSec_blob--br"></span>
      <img class="twsSec_img" src="About_image/res25.png" alt="TWS technician on an occupied maintenance site">
    </div>
    <div class="twsSec_body">
      <p class="twsSec_p">TWS Service &amp; Solutions works with clients who want that kind of contractor: responsive, practical, careful with occupied spaces, and realistic about cost. The goal is not to make every property look brand new. Some buildings do not need that. The goal is to keep assets safe, presentable, compliant, usable, and less likely to surprise the people responsible for them.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">For clients looking for a <a href="https://www.twsss.com.au"  style="text-decoration: none;    color: black;"> <b>Trusted facility contractor Sydney</b></a> teams can deal with directly, our role is to make maintenance easier to manage without pretending it is ever completely effortless.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">Good work still needs access. It needs accurate information. It needs approval before a small problem becomes a larger one. It needs tenants to report faults early instead of waiting until water reaches the carpet. It needs owners to accept that deferred maintenance is not saving money; it is usually moving the cost to a worse day.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p">TWS Service &amp; Solutions understands that balance. Spend where the risk justifies it. Repair where repair is still honest. Replace when keeping the old asset alive has become false economy.</p>
      <p class="twsSec_p"><strong style="color:var(--twsSec-green);">That is how a <a href="https://www.twsss.com.au"  style="text-decoration: none;    color: #1a8043;"> <b>facility maintenance company NSW</b></a> should operate.</strong></p>
      <a class="twsSec_cta" href="https://www.twsss.com.au/contact">
        Request an honest assessment
        <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>





